ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Cervical Cancer - Status: Exacerbated (Acute Haemorrhage and Suspected Obstructive Uropathy)
Differentials:

Uterine Fibroids (Leiomyoma) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the presence of foul-smelling discharge, severe flank pain (suggesting ureteral invasion), and significant systemic weight loss.

Pelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: the sheer volume of haemorrhage, presence of right flank pain indicating hydronephrosis, and lack of high systemic fever.

Ectopic Pregnancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: patient age/history of chronic foul discharge and weight loss preceding the acute event, though a beta-hCG must be performed in ED to definitively exclude.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is exhibiting signs of decompensated hypovolemic shock (hypotension, marked tachycardia, diaphoresis) secondary to severe, acute vaginal haemorrhage. The preceding history of foul discharge, postcoital bleeding, and new right flank pain strongly suggests locally advanced cervical malignancy complicated by acute tumour bed haemorrhage and ureteral obstruction (hydronephrosis). Delaying assessment until normal clinic hours would guarantee profound hypovolemic shock, potential renal failure, and death. This presentation absolutely mandates an urgent after-hours visit to recognize the shock state and facilitate immediate emergency medical transport.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate positioning flat with legs elevated. Urgent dispatch of emergency ambulance (000) for immediate ED transfer for fluid resuscitation, blood transfusion, urgent pelvic examination (anticipating a friable exophytic mass), and urgent renal ultrasound.
Positive Behavioral Change:

Remain strictly flat in bed with your legs elevated on pillows. By using gravity to keep blood flowing to your brain and vital organs, you reduce the risk of fainting and organ damage.

Do not insert tampons or anything else internally. By keeping the area clear, you prevent further trauma to the actively bleeding tissue and reduce infection risk.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Immediate ED transfer overrides routine GP follow-up.

